## What happens

In `packages/ai/src/api/anthropic-messages.ts`, when the provider closes the stream without naming a stop reason:

```ts
if (output.stopReason === "pending")
    throw new Error("Anthropic stream ended without a stop reason");
```

`pending` means no `message_delta` carried a stop reason — but the content has already arrived by that point. `output` holds the accumulated text, thinking and tool calls, and the provider has already counted and charged for the tokens. The throw makes all of that unreachable, so a generation the provider billed is thrown away with the error.

There is a second throw for the same condition, earlier in the stream:

```ts
if (sawMessageStart && !sawMessageEnd)
    throw new Error("Anthropic stream ended before message_stop");
```

Both discard the accumulated message in the same way — the `error` event carries it, and the agent loop then drops it.

## Why it costs more than the answer

Pi records the failed message and omits it from the model's context. That is right for a half-written answer — but the rewound prefix is what the prompt cache was keyed on, so the next round re-sends the whole conversation cold.

Measured on 2026-11-18, in one session of 256 rounds:

| | |
|---|---|
| rounds that ended with `Anthropic stream ended without a stop reason` | 4 of 256 |
| characters of thinking already streamed when they died | 49,382 · 61,047 · 2,708 · 18,397 |
| tokens pi recorded for them | 0 each — the accounting cannot see them |
| the two rounds after them | 776,910 input tokens with 182 cached, then 799,358 with 256 |
| uncached input tokens in 90 seconds | 1.58M, at the doubled rate that applies above 512k |

The model then re-derived the same plan from scratch, four times, each time opening with the identical sentence *"Now I have a comprehensive picture"*. The session spent 2h26m and 67.2M tokens (64.1M of them cache reads) and produced no code. The re-runs were the largest single item in the bill.

## A note on the stop reason

A salvage must **not** use `stopReason: "error"`. Pi drops any assistant message whose stop reason is `"error"` or `"aborted"` from the model's context (`transformMessages` in `packages/ai`) and ends the turn on it (`runLoop` in `packages/agent-core`) — so an `"error"` salvage discards the answer exactly as the throw does.

`"length"` is the stop reason pi keeps. The message stays in the context, and the agent loop already refuses to run a tool call whose arguments were cut short (`failToolCallsFromTruncatedMessage`), telling the model the output was cut.

Worth knowing when choosing it: a message with stop reason `"length"` whose output is below the model's maximum is treated as a *recoverable* truncation (`isRecoverableLength`), and pi then compacts the context and retries. That is pi's designed answer to "the output was cut", and it is a better one than discarding the answer and re-sending the whole context cold — but it is a compaction and a retry, so it is worth being deliberate about.

## Suggested change

Keep what arrived, and name it. The catch block is the right place, because it is the one place that has both the error and the accumulated message:

```ts
// packages/ai/src/api/anthropic-messages.ts

/** The provider closed the stream without saying why. */
const UNNAMED_STOP_REASON = /ended (?:before message_stop|without a stop reason)/i;

function hasContent(message: AssistantMessage): boolean {
    return message.content.some((block) =>
        block.type === "text" ? block.text.trim().length > 0
        : block.type === "thinking" ? block.thinking.trim().length > 0
        : block.type === "toolCall");
}

// ... in the stream's catch block:

} catch (error) {
    // The provider closed the stream without naming a stop reason. The
    // content has already arrived and the provider has already charged for
    // it, so failing here throws away an answer that was paid for — and
    // rewinds the prompt cache, so the next round re-sends the whole
    // context cold.
    //
    // "length" is the stop reason pi keeps: a message whose stop reason is
    // "error" or "aborted" is dropped from the model's context
    // (transformMessages) and ends the turn (runLoop). "length" is also pi's
    // own mark for an answer that was cut, so the agent loop already refuses
    // to run a tool call whose arguments were cut short.
    if (UNNAMED_STOP_REASON.test(error instanceof Error ? error.message : "") && hasContent(output)) {
        output.stopReason = "length";
        output.errorMessage = undefined;
        stream2.push({ type: "done", reason: "length", message: output });
        stream2.end();
        return;
    }
    output.stopReason = options?.signal?.aborted ? "aborted" : "error";
    output.errorMessage = error instanceof Error ? error.message : JSON.stringify(error);
    stream2.push({ type: "error", reason: output.stopReason, error: output });
    stream2.end();
}
```

## The same shape is in the other implementations

This is not only in the Anthropic Messages implementation. Every provider that ends its stream on a stop reason it was told has the same guard:

| file | message |
|---|---|
| `api/anthropic-messages.ts` | `Anthropic stream ended without a stop reason` |
| `api/azure-openai-responses.ts` | `Azure OpenAI Responses stream ended without a stop reason` |
| `api/bedrock-converse-stream.ts` | `Bedrock stream ended without a stop reason` |
| `api/google-generative-ai.ts` | `Google stream ended without a finish reason` |
| `api/pi-messages.ts` | `Faux response ended without a stop reason` |

If there is a shared layer the salvage could live in, that would cover all of them at once — the agent loop receives the accumulated message either way. Otherwise each implementation wants the same catch-block change, and the matcher has to know about "finish reason" as well as "stop reason".

## How to reproduce

A provider that speaks the Anthropic Messages protocol and closes the stream without naming a stop reason is enough. `message_start`, a `content_block_start`/`delta`/`stop` for some text, and then either no `message_delta`, or no `message_stop`:

```
event: message_start
data: {"type":"message_start","message":{"id":"msg_1","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"m","content":[],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1234,"output_tokens":0}}}

event: content_block_start
data: {"type":"content_block_start","index":0,"content_block":{"type":"text","text":""}}

event: content_block_delta
data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"Now I have a comprehensive picture"}}

event: content_block_stop
data: {"type":"content_block_stop","index":0}

event: message_stop
data: {"type":"message_stop"}
```

The stream ends there. Pi reports `Anthropic stream ended without a stop reason`, and the text above is gone — although it was in the message the error carried.

### What happened?

If an extension starts a run during startup (`sendUserMessage`, or `sendMessage` with `triggerTurn`, from `session_start` or `resources_discover`), the run starts before Pi applies the skills and prompts that `resources_discover` handlers return. The run keeps its starting prompt options, so it never gets those skills.

I hit it in an extension that starts child sessions with a task: their first run had none of the `pi-claude-marketplace` skills. Starting the run from `resources_discover`, as suggested in #11268, is still too early.

### Steps to reproduce

```
d=$(mktemp -d) && cd "$d"
mkdir probe-skill
printf -- '---\nname: probe-skill\ndescription: probe\n---\n' > probe-skill/SKILL.md
cat > a.ts <<EOF
// contributes a skill a bit late
export default function (pi) {
  pi.on("resources_discover", async () => {
    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 300));
    return { skillPaths: ["$d/probe-skill"] };
  });
}
EOF
cat > b.ts <<'EOF'
// starts a run during startup and logs its skills, then checks the prompt again later
export default function (pi) {
  pi.on("session_start", (_e, ctx) => {
    pi.sendUserMessage("hi");
    setTimeout(() => console.error("later:", ctx.getSystemPrompt().includes("probe-skill")), 1000);
  });
  pi.on("before_agent_start", (e, ctx) => {
    console.error("run:", e.systemPromptOptions.skills.map((s) => s.name));
    ctx.abort();
  });
}
EOF
sleep 2 | pi --mode rpc --no-session --no-extensions --no-skills -e ./b.ts -e ./a.ts > /dev/null
```

Prints `run: []` and `later: true`. Same with `-e ./a.ts -e ./b.ts`, and when `b.ts` sends from `resources_discover`.

### Expected behavior

Pi holds a run that an extension starts during startup until discovered resources are applied, like it does for `pi "<prompt>"`. If that is not wanted, a `session_ready` event after resources are applied (#11268) would let extensions wait for it.

### Version

1.5.2

### What happened?

When running [boadij/pi-file](https://github.com/boadij/pi-file), I sometimes experience a delay after pressing Enter before the generated `<file>` tags appear in the editor.

The likely cause is that `pasteToEditor()` mutates editor state without requesting a repaint. Normal keyboard input explicitly schedules rendering, but extension-driven editor mutations do not.

This appears related to #5620. A similar issue was fixed in [oh-my-pi PR #4342](https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/pull/4342).

### Steps to reproduce

Create `repro.ts`:

```ts
export default function (pi: any) {
  pi.registerCommand("repro", {
    handler: async (_args: string, ctx: any) => {
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 200));
      ctx.ui.pasteToEditor("Hello from extension");
    },
  });
}
```

Run `pi -ne -e ./repro.ts` and enter `/repro`.

The suspected failure is that the editor contents change but remain visually stale until another input or render event occurs.

This is a minimal reproduction candidate, not independently validated.

### Expected behavior

`pasteToEditor()` and `setEditorText()` should automatically request a repaint whenever they modify the editor, including after asynchronous operations. Extensions should not need their own rendering workarounds.

### Version

1.5.2

**Version:** pi-coding-agent 1.0.0, pi-ai 1.0.0 (Node 26). Model: an Anthropic Messages model with a 400,000-token window and a 65,536-token output cap, with a server tool in the request (seen with the advisor tool, `advisor_20260301`).

## Summary

Three behaviors combine into an agent that cannot answer:

1. **Summed usage is taken as context.** When a response ran a server tool, Anthropic's `message_delta.usage` holds `iterations`, and the top-level `input_tokens`, `cache_read_input_tokens` and `cache_creation_input_tokens` are the sum over all executor steps of that response. `pi-ai/dist/api/anthropic-messages.js` (the `message_delta` branch, about line 621) copies those top-level fields into `usage` and sets `totalTokens = input + output + cacheRead + cacheWrite`. With one server tool call at about 200K of real context, the reply records about 400K. `utils/estimate.js` `estimateContextTokens` then uses that `totalTokens` as the current context size.
2. **The inflated reading never goes away after length recovery.** The next request is clamped (point 3) and comes back empty with `stopReason: "length"` and 1 output token. `core/agent-session.js` treats it as `isRecoverableLength` (about line 2339) and calls `_omitRecoveryAttempt`, which writes a `context_edit` with `replacement: null`. That removes the one fresh, correct usage reading, so the inflated reply stays the latest usage source and every retry is clamped the same way. If the overflow compaction that follows also fails, nothing resets it: the loop repeats on every message, and a reload rebuilds the same messages.
3. **The clamp floors at 1.** `api/simple-options.js` `clampMaxTokensToContext` computes `contextWindow - estimate - CONTEXT_SAFETY_TOKENS (4096)` and returns `Math.max(MIN_MAX_TOKENS, available)` with `MIN_MAX_TOKENS = 1`. When the estimate exceeds the window it sends `max_tokens: 1` instead of reporting an overflow.

## Minimal repro (offline, Pi's own functions)

```js
const base = '<pi-ai>/dist/';
const { clampMaxTokensToContext } = await import(base + 'api/simple-options.js');
const { estimateContextTokens } = await import(base + 'utils/estimate.js');
const model = { contextWindow: 400000, maxTokens: 65536 };
const usage = (cacheRead, cacheWrite, output) => ({ input: 4, output, cacheRead, cacheWrite,
  totalTokens: 4 + output + cacheRead + cacheWrite, cost: { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0, total: 0 } });
const assistant = (u, t) => ({ role: 'assistant', content: [{ type: 'text', text: 'ok' }], stopReason: 'toolUse', usage: u, timestamp: t });
const user = t => ({ role: 'user', content: 'next', timestamp: t });
// The reply that called a server tool: two executor steps of about 200K each, summed by the API.
const summed = [user(1), assistant(usage(400000, 2000, 400), 2), user(3)];
// The same reply with the last step's usage.
const lastStep = [user(1), assistant(usage(201000, 2000, 400), 2), user(3)];
console.log(estimateContextTokens({ messages: summed }).tokens, clampMaxTokensToContext(model, { messages: summed }, 65536));
// 402,405 -> 1
console.log(estimateContextTokens({ messages: lastStep }).tokens, clampMaxTokensToContext(model, { messages: lastStep }, 65536));
// 203,405 -> 65536
```

Live: in a session at about 200K of context, a reply that calls a server tool records about 400K of usage. Every later request is sent with `max_tokens: 1` and returns an empty `length` stop; Pi omits it and compacts, and if that compaction fails the agent stays stuck.

Observed numbers from one such reply: top-level `cache_read_input_tokens` 400,506 and `cache_creation_input_tokens` 2,484, while the next request (same history plus one short message) read 201,062 from cache. The first executor step had read the previous reply's whole 199,444-token prompt from cache, so 400,506 = 199,444 + 201,062.

## Suggested fixes

1. In the Anthropic provider, when `usage.iterations` is present, take `input`, `cacheRead` and `cacheWrite` from the last executor step (`type: "message"`) for the context reading, and keep the summed values (or the reported cost) for billing. For example: keep `cost` from the top-level usage, set `totalTokens` from the last step's prompt plus the response's output.
2. After `_omitRecoveryAttempt` on a length stop, do not let an older reading stay the only usage source: either keep the omitted reply's usage for estimation, or fall back to a character estimate for the context.
3. In `clampMaxTokensToContext`, when `available` is below a usable floor, raise a context-overflow error (the existing `isContextOverflow` path) instead of sending `max_tokens: 1`.

Any one of these breaks the loop; (1) removes the wrong reading at its source.

## Still present in 1.5.2

Checked against the published 1.5.2 packages: `pi-ai/dist/api/simple-options.js` still has `MIN_MAX_TOKENS = 1` (lines 11-22; the margin is now `max(4096, input x ratio)`), `pi-ai/dist/api/anthropic-messages.js` still copies the top-level `cache_read_input_tokens` on `message_delta` (about line 844) with no `iterations` handling, and `pi-coding-agent/dist/core/agent-session.js` still omits the recoverable length attempt (about lines 2533-2564).

Related, closed as not planned: #11988 (clamp to 1 when the estimate exceeds the window), #11236. This report adds the cause on the Anthropic side: summed `iterations` usage after a server tool call.

### What happened?

We embed the coding-agent SDK in a long-running server ([OAR](https://github.com/botiverse/oar)): one Node process hosts many `AgentSession`s, and a session stays open for days instead of being restarted. A session's memory only grows. `SessionManager` keeps every entry in `fileEntries` / `byId` for the session's lifetime, compaction shrinks the LLM context but frees nothing, and tool-result images stay there as base64. Resuming parses the whole JSONL into memory again.

In the CLI this ends with the process. Embedded, every session adds to one heap, and one large session can take the whole host down with an out-of-memory crash. Same root cause as #6841 and #9613, which were auto-closed without review.

### Steps to reproduce

No model calls; pi 1.1.0, Node 24, heap measured after `gc()`:

1. `SessionManager.create(cwd, dir)`; append 500 tool results with 200 KB of text each and 100 with a ~1 MB base64 image.
2. `appendCompaction(...)`; `buildSessionContext()` now returns 2 messages.
3. Heap: +197 MB after appending, still +197 MB after compaction. `SessionManager.open()` on the 195 MB file adds another +195 MB.

<details><summary>Script</summary>

```js
// node --expose-gc repro.mjs
import { SessionManager } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
import { mkdtempSync } from "node:fs";
const heap = () => (gc(), process.memoryUsage().heapUsed / 2 ** 20 | 0);
const dir = mkdtempSync("/tmp/pi-mem-"), base = heap(), sm = SessionManager.create("/tmp", dir);
const usage = { input: 1, output: 1, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0, totalTokens: 2, cost: { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0, total: 0 } };
sm.appendMessage({ role: "user", content: "start", timestamp: Date.now() });
let last = sm.appendMessage({ role: "assistant", content: [{ type: "text", text: "ok" }], api: "openai-completions", provider: "t", model: "t", usage, stopReason: "stop", timestamp: Date.now() });
const text = "x".repeat(200 * 1024), image = Buffer.alloc(750 * 1024, 7).toString("base64");
for (let i = 0; i < 500; i++) last = sm.appendMessage({ role: "toolResult", toolCallId: `c${i}`, toolName: "bash", content: [{ type: "text", text: text + i }], isError: false, timestamp: Date.now() });
for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) last = sm.appendMessage({ role: "toolResult", toolCallId: `i${i}`, toolName: "shot", content: [{ type: "image", data: image + i, mimeType: "image/png" }], isError: false, timestamp: Date.now() });
console.log("appended", heap() - base, "MB");
sm.appendCompaction("summary", last, 1);
console.log("compacted", heap() - base, "MB, context", sm.buildSessionContext().messages.length);
const reopened = SessionManager.open(sm.getSessionFile(), dir);
console.log("after reopen", heap() - base, "MB, context", reopened.buildSessionContext().messages.length);
```
</details>

### Expected behavior

A session's memory bounded by what its context needs, or at least dropping after compaction, with images not kept in memory. pi-durable already works this way: entries are committed to storage, and only the latest context view stays in memory, released after `contextRetentionMs` when idle. Is that where coding-agent sessions are heading? If so we would rather wait for it than work around it. If not, would a `SessionManager` change (lazy entries, images stored outside the entries) be welcome? We can test it in a long-running embedding.

### Version

1.1.0

## What happened?

In the Web Shell (Qwen Code Desktop and the standalone `qwen serve` web mode), the **Changes panel** (the git diff dialog, `GitDiffDialog`) renders long diff lines with a per-line horizontal scrollbar:

`packages/web-shell/client/components/dialogs/GitDiffDialog.module.css` (v0.27.2):

```css
.diffContent {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-width: 0;
  white-space: pre;
  overflow-x: auto;
  padding-right: 8px;
}
```

`white-space: pre` keeps every diff line on a single line and `overflow-x: auto` gives each long line its own horizontal scroller, so any line wider than the panel is clipped and can only be read by scrolling that specific line horizontally.

## What did you expect to happen?

Long lines wrap inside the panel (line numbers and the +/- markers stay in place), so the diff reads top-to-bottom without per-line horizontal scrolling.

## Notes

- #13248 covered the same rendering problem in the **tool cards** (WriteFile/Edit, `DiffView.module.css`) and was closed 2026-10-30 by #13272, which changed only `.content` in `DiffView.module.css`. The Changes panel is a second diff renderer and was not covered — the snippet above is the current state in the v0.27.2 tag.
- A two-property change looks sufficient (mirrors the #13272 fix):

```css
.diffContent {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-width: 0;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  padding-right: 8px;
}
```

`pre-wrap` preserves intra-line spacing and indentation; `overflow-wrap: anywhere` covers unbreakable tokens (long paths, URLs).

### Summary

Record the agent that an inbound A2A task was routed to on the task itself (A2A `Task.metadata`), so peers and operators can see it from `SendMessage`/`GetTask`.

### Problem to solve

An A2A `Task` returned by `SendMessage`/`GetTask` carries `id`, `contextId`, `status`, `artifacts` and an always-empty `history`. With several agents and bindings, neither the peer nor the operator can tell from the task which agent the route resolved to, or which session ran it. Today the only way to find out is to search each agent's sessions with `chat.history` for the message.

In the v2026.11.5 source, `A2aTaskStore.create` (`extensions/a2a/src/task-store.ts:85-103`) builds the task before routing. The routing result (`route.agentId`, `route.sessionKey`) is only known later, in `dispatchA2aInbound` (`extensions/a2a/src/inbound.ts:33-43`), and is never written back to the task.

### Proposed solution

After routing, record the resolved agent on the task as A2A `metadata` (the 1.0 `Task` has a `metadata` map). Sketch:

```ts
// extensions/a2a/src/inbound.ts, after resolveAgentRoute(...)
params.store.annotate(params.taskId, {
  openclaw: { agentId: route.agentId },
});

// A2aTaskStore
annotate(taskId: string, metadata: Record<string, unknown>): void {
  const task = this.#tasks.get(taskId);
  if (task && !isTerminalTask(task)) {
    task.metadata = { ...task.metadata, ...metadata };
  }
}
```

The Agent Card already lists agent ids as `skills`, so the value matches something clients already know. If the session key is better kept gateway-side, `agentId` on the task plus an operator gateway method (for example `a2a.tasks.get` returning the task and its `sessionKey`) would cover both needs.

### Alternatives considered

- Searching `chat.history` of every agent's sessions for the peer's `messageId`: slow, and it depends on history retention and on message size (large messages lose `__openclaw.transport` in the oversized placeholder).
- Inferring the agent from bindings in the config: breaks as soon as bindings change, and is exactly what we want to confirm.

### Impact

Affected: operators running several agents behind the A2A channel, and A2A peers talking to them.
Severity: annoying (debugging routing takes manual searching).
Frequency: every time routing needs checking, for example after changing a binding.
Consequence: extra manual work to confirm which agent answered a task.

### Evidence/examples

The A2A 1.0 `Task` object has an optional `metadata` map intended for this kind of implementation-specific detail.

### Do you plan to open a PR for this?

Maybe, with maintainer guidance

### Additional information

Small related point: `history` is always `[]` (`task-store.ts:97`), and `historyLength` in `GetTask`/`SendMessage` params is accepted but not used. Either filling it or documenting that it is always empty would help clients.

### Bug type

Behavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)

### Beta release blocker

No

### Summary

Compaction parses a session's entire transcript onto the main thread, so a long-lived session lifts the gateway heap by about 1 GB on every compaction

### Steps to reproduce

**Minimal repro (2026.10.9, about 15 minutes, needs about 3 GB RAM)**
1. `seed.cjs <state>/agents/main/sessions agent:main:big 575 <workspace>` writes one session of plain user / assistant / tool-call / tool-result turns (tool results 2–190 KB of random text) until it reaches 575 MB (22,273 events). Then `openclaw doctor --session-sqlite import --session-sqlite-all-agents` imports it.
2. Config: one OpenAI-compatible provider pointed at `mock-slow.cjs` (instant replies for the agent model, a summary model `summ` that streams for 45 s), `agents.defaults.compaction = { mode: "safeguard", model: "fixture/summ", maxActiveTranscriptBytes: "2mb" }`.
3. Start `openclaw gateway run` with `-r preload-mem.cjs` in NODE_OPTIONS (logs `process.memoryUsage()` and V8 heap spaces every second, and writes a heap snapshot on request).
4. `openclaw agent --session-key agent:main:big -m "hello"`. The preflight compaction starts at once.
5. Repeat with the session seeded at 50 MB.

