## What Problem This Solves

When the Gateway service exits during startup, `openclaw gateway start` can keep waiting for HTTP readiness until the full startup deadline, even after the service check confirms it stopped and freed its port.

Closes #201607

## Why This Change Was Made

The startup checks still run together, but a terminal `stopped-free` service result now cancels the HTTP readiness probe. Normal startup still requires both service health and successful `/healthz` and `/readyz` responses.

## User Impact

The start command now reports the service failure promptly instead of appearing hung while probing an unreachable Gateway.

## Evidence

- Production-module-boundary proof exercises `runDaemonStart` and the real HTTP readiness implementation against a closed loopback port. The Windows service result is controlled at the service adapter boundary because this host is macOS.
- The same regression stayed pending on base `799c2eb91295b2f777fa649c20d4fc31d81a84ba`; on head `b0ddcbeb6ff58f70e7d19fdb60414e00921d2335`, it returned the expected start failure before the one-second guard.
- Negative control: all 13 lifecycle readiness tests passed, including the Windows cold-start allowance. The existing `waitForGatewayHttpReadiness` helper continues to honor cancellation signals.

```text
$ pnpm exec vitest run src/cli/daemon-cli/lifecycle-readiness.test.ts -t 'settles start when the managed service is stopped before HTTP readiness'
before: base 799c2eb — start remained pending through the 1-second guard (test runner exit 1)
after: head b0ddcbe — 1 passed; observed start failure exit 1 before the guard (test runner exit 0)
$ pnpm exec vitest run src/cli/daemon-cli/lifecycle-readiness.test.ts
negative-control: head b0ddcbe — 13 passed; Windows cold-start allowance passed
```

The issue reports the Windows 11 Scheduled Task failure. Native Windows execution was unavailable on this macOS host.

